- op: discuss  # b8f0
  at: '2026-11-13T07:22:30Z'  # b8f0
  by: agent  # b8f0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b8f0
  clock: 6  # b8f0
  nonce: b8f0  # b8f0
  message: "DONE in PR #2163 (dev 065482571d; code 7a8ccdf094). ROOT CAUSE: every test->block pair was stored as three strings (about 236 bytes) and held again in the primary-key index, so the cost of any cold access scaled with a 3.8 GB file. FIX: format 2 stores each test, file and block once and keeps pairs as two integers (WITHOUT ROWID, second index by block); PRAGMA user_version marks the format; open_index converts a format-1 file without running tests (new file beside the old, pair-count check, hard link to <name>.format-1, atomic replace, under flock) and refuses an unknown format; the two narrowing queries are index lookups, pinned by their query plans. ACCEPTANCE (coverage-select under 1 minute from a cold cache), measured on the merged tree with the real index evicted by posix_fadvise DONTNEED: coverage-select narrow (4 changed files, 217-file run set) 2.61 s reading 64 MB; coverage-select select 2.31 s reading 2.8 MB. BEFORE, same inputs, format-1 copy, old code: narrow 14.9 s reading 1.62 GB. Update after a 3,166-test run, on a copy: 19.4 s / 287 MB read / 587 MB written before; 11.2 s / 32 MB / 55 MB after. SIZE: 3,838,394,368 B -> 209,010,688 B; conversion 42.5 s on a copy. EQUIVALENCE: 4,515,246 pairs identical as a multiset on the copy; blocks, unmeasured tests, meta and read tables identical; old code on format 1 and new code on format 2 gave identical select and narrow output on 60 commits (5 narrowed, 55 refused); touching sets identical for all 1,280 files with pairs and block test sets for all 15,376 blocks; on the real index after conversion 34,005 tests have edges identical to the backup and the 297 that differ all ran in the PR's own test run. 12 mutations of the pinned behaviours each turned a test red. NOT ASSERTED: that the 2026-10-06 stall is reproduced and cured. It did not reproduce before the change: tonight random 4 KB direct reads took 0.04-0.2 ms against the 4-20 ms recorded then, and the format-1 file was already under a minute cold. The claim is the I/O volume (about 25x less read per narrow, about 10x less written per update), which is what a slow disk multiplies. LEFT IN PLACE: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/selection_index.sqlite.format-1 (3.8 GB, the old index), for the owner to delete. A checkout still on pre-#2163 code cannot read the converted index: its coverage-select fails and smart-test falls back to the import-graph selection."  # b8f0
